<%# ── TIMELINE ── %>
<div class="nat-section">
  <div class="nat-section-hdr">◆ Timeline</div>
  <div class="nat-section-title">Key spending milestones</div>
  <div class="nat-timeline">
    <div class="nat-tl-item">
      <div class="nat-tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="nat-tl-year">2001</div>
      <p class="nat-tl-text">9/11 and anthrax attacks. Emergency supplemental: <strong>$40B approved within days.</strong> USPS begins BDS engineering. Five postal workers die; Brentwood facility shut down.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="nat-tl-item">
      <div class="nat-tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="nat-tl-year">2002</div>
      <p class="nat-tl-text"><strong>DHS created.</strong> Largest U.S. government reorganization since WWII. 22 agencies merged. Pilot BDS installed in Baltimore. National defense spending surges 50% vs. 2001 baseline.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="nat-tl-item">
      <div class="nat-tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="nat-tl-year">2003</div>
      <p class="nat-tl-text">Iraq War begins. <strong>742-unit BDS contract</strong> awarded to Northrop Grumman. TSA full-body scanner program begins development.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="nat-tl-item">
      <div class="nat-tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="nat-tl-year">2005</div>
      <p class="nat-tl-text"><strong>BDS network complete.</strong> All 282 USPS large facilities equipped. 27 billion mail pieces screened without a false positive. Annual cartridge spend: ~$57M to Cepheid alone.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="nat-tl-item">
      <div class="nat-tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="nat-tl-year">2008</div>
      <p class="nat-tl-text"><strong>Spending peak: $260B in a single year.</strong> 16× the pre-9/11 counter-terrorism baseline. Height of simultaneous Afghanistan and Iraq operations.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="nat-tl-item">
      <div class="nat-tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="nat-tl-year">2013</div>
      <p class="nat-tl-text">Snowden disclosures. <strong>NSA budget partially revealed: ~$52B annual National Intelligence Program.</strong> First public accounting of bulk surveillance costs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="nat-tl-item">
      <div class="nat-tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="nat-tl-year">2017</div>
      <p class="nat-tl-text">Stimson Center report. First independent accounting of 15-year CT spend: <strong>$2.8T (2002–2017).</strong> Homeland security alone: $979B. Finding: the U.S. lacked any official government-wide tally.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="nat-tl-item">
      <div class="nat-tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="nat-tl-year">2021</div>
      <p class="nat-tl-text">Afghanistan withdrawal. Brown University final tally: <strong>$8T total</strong> when veterans care and interest are included. 20-year Afghanistan cost alone exceeded $2T.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<%# ── COST-EFFECTIVENESS CALLOUT ── %>
<div class="nat-section" style="padding-bottom:40px;">
  <div class="nat-section-hdr">◆ Cost-effectiveness</div>
  <div class="nat-callout">
    <div class="nat-callout-title">How to think about cost-effectiveness</div>
    <p>
      The Stimson Center estimated that for CT spending to "break even" in lives-saved
      terms — matching its cost against the value of a statistical human life — it would
      have needed to prevent <strong>188,740 deaths, or about 11,796 per year.</strong>
      Actual jihadi-caused deaths on U.S. soil from 2002–2017: 172 total.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's not an argument the spending was wasted — deterrence and disrupted plots are
      inherently unquantifiable. But it does illustrate the honest difficulty of the
      cost-benefit question. In the same period, opioid overdoses killed more than
      <strong>20,000 Americans in a single year (2016)</strong> with a fraction of the
      federal response.
    </p>
    <p>
      The deeper accounting problem: the U.S. government still does not publish a unified
      counter-terrorism spending total. This page is an effort toward that transparency.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

